      "=The Hoard of the Unflown — Lore",
      "=The Guild Ledger",
      "=Guild Ledger, the ledger, guild, Quill's debt, buy out, buying out, the guild",
      "=The Thieves' Guild of the city keeps no coin — it keeps a ledger, and the ledger keeps everyone. Every job a thief runs is entered as a debt: tools advanced, fences owed, protection rendered, names bought. You do not pay the ledger off with money alone; you pay it off with jobs, and each job adds a line.\n\nQuill's name sits deep in the ledger. She has run more jobs than most thieves see in a lifetime and owes more at the end of them than at the start — the guild's arithmetic is designed that way. The only way out is a single score large enough to settle every line at once, and the guild knows it, so it prices such settlements high and grudgingly.\n\nGuild rules that matter to this story: a thief may not take a commission that harms the guild's interests; a thief may not reveal a client's name under any torture short of death; a thief who runs a job without guild sanction forfeits all standing and every debt comes due at once. A dragon as a client is unprecedented, and the guild has not yet decided whether to be insulted, delighted, or afraid.\n\nThe ledger is also leverage. Anyone who can read it knows who owes what to whom — which is why the guild keeps it in a place no thief has ever successfully robbed, and why a knight with enough coin might buy a page of it.",
      "=on",
      "Why not="
    ]
  }
}
